1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,320
Japan built a robot with
a CHAINSAW for an arm.

2
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:06,890
And this is real footage of it
cutting branches beside a railway.

3
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:08,160
Watch the other hand.

4
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,980
It grabs the branch
before the saw touches it.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,330
As the blade cuts through, that
claw is still holding the wood.

6
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:17,955
That's important, because a falling
branch and train tracks are a terrible

7
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:18,900
combination.

8
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:21,440
But now look at the rest of the robot.

9
00:00:21,670 --> 00:00:22,900
It doesn't have legs.

10
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,310
It's attached to a massive boom,

11
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:27,540
rising out of a railway
maintenance vehicle.

12
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:29,510
And it's not deciding where to cut.

13
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:32,220
A PERSON is controlling every movement.

14
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:36,525
From a cabin below, the operator watches
through the robot's cameras and moves

15
00:00:36,545 --> 00:00:38,380
its arms using hand controls.

16
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,900
It looks like a giant humanoid robot,

17
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:43,720
but the actual intelligence
is sitting at the bottom.

18
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,030
And those two arms aren't just for show.

19
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:49,400
One grips the branch and takes its weight.

20
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:51,960
The other angles the
chainsaw into the cut.

21
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:55,740
As the blade breaks through, the claw
can keep holding the branch instead of

22
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:57,500
letting it drop onto the railway.

23
00:00:57,810 --> 00:00:59,420
A crane can lift something.

24
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:01,230
A chainsaw can cut something.

25
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:03,460
This machine is designed to do BOTH,

26
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:06,400
at the same time, under
one person's control.

27
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,970
But there's another piece of engineering
you can't see in the footage.

28
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,600
The Japanese robotics company behind
this kind of machine developed something

29
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:15,500
called bilateral control.

30
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:19,650
Tiny movements at the operator's controls
command much larger forces at the

31
00:01:19,670 --> 00:01:20,320
robot.

32
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:24,270
And resistance at the robot can be
transmitted back through those controls,

33
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:27,580
so the person can actually sense
how hard the machine is pushing.

34
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:30,660
Imagine touching a tree
branch with your fingertips.

35
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:34,110
Except your fingers are
now giant STEEL ARMS.

36
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:38,230
And this isn't just a robotics stunt.

37
00:01:38,430 --> 00:01:41,610
Railway workers have to maintain
equipment high above the tracks,

38
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:43,920
sometimes near overhead
electrical hardware.

39
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:46,520
That means climbing, handling heavy tools,

40
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:48,330
and controlling things that can fall.

41
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,180
Moving the operator away from the
dangerous part of the job changes the

42
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:52,750
risk.

43
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:56,790
That's one reason Japan's railways are
putting machines like this to work.

44
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:00,400
JR East introduced this type
of machine earlier this year.

45
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:04,810
And JR West, which was already
using a related system in 2024,

46
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:08,840
is rolling out an improved humanoid
model with an even longer reach.

47
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:12,755
It's also introducing a separate
single-arm robot built for lifting heavy

48
00:02:12,775 --> 00:02:14,020
railway components.

49
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:18,150
The goal is safer maintenance, with
fewer people needed for each job.

50
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:22,430
And there's STILL a human
controlling that chainsaw.

51
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:24,770
That might actually be
the most interesting part.

52
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:28,670
Japan didn't give a robot a chainsaw
and tell it to go find trees.

53
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:32,000
It gave a PERSON a pair
of giant mechanical arms.
